#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-gc(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:7
msgid "git-gc - Cleanup unnecessary files and optimize the local repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:13
msgid ""
"'git gc' [--aggressive] [--auto] [--[no-]detach] [--quiet] [--prune=<date> | "
"--no-prune] [--force] [--keep-largest-pack]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:22
msgid ""
"Runs a number of housekeeping tasks within the current repository, such as "
"compressing file revisions (to reduce disk space and increase performance), "
"removing unreachable objects which may have been created from prior "
"invocations of 'git add', packing refs, pruning reflog, rerere metadata or "
"stale working trees. May also update ancillary indexes such as the "
"commit-graph."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:27
msgid ""
"When common porcelain operations that create objects are run, they will "
"check whether the repository has grown substantially since the last "
"maintenance, and if so run `git gc` automatically. See `gc.auto` below for "
"how to disable this behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:33
msgid ""
"Running `git gc` manually should only be needed when adding objects to a "
"repository without regularly running such porcelain commands, to do a "
"one-off repository optimization, or e.g. to clean up a suboptimal "
"mass-import. See the \"PACKFILE OPTIMIZATION\" section in "
"linkgit:git-fast-import[1] for more details on the import case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:43
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--aggressive::`\n"
"\tUsually 'git gc' runs very quickly while providing good disk\n"
"\tspace utilization and performance.  This option will cause\n"
"\t'git gc' to more aggressively optimize the repository at the expense\n"
"\tof taking much more time.  The effects of this optimization are\n"
"\tmostly persistent. See the \"AGGRESSIVE\" section below for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--auto::`\n"
"\tWith this option, 'git gc' checks whether any housekeeping is\n"
"\trequired; if not, it exits without performing any work.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:50
msgid ""
"See the `gc.auto` option in the \"CONFIGURATION\" section below for how this "
"heuristic works."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:55
msgid ""
"Once housekeeping is triggered by exceeding the limits of configuration "
"options such as `gc.auto` and `gc.autoPackLimit`, all other housekeeping "
"tasks (e.g. rerere, working trees, reflog...) will be performed as well."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--[no-]detach::`\n"
"\tRun in the background if the system supports it. This option overrides\n"
"\tthe `gc.autoDetach` config.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--[no-]cruft::`\n"
"\tWhen expiring unreachable objects, pack them separately into a\n"
"\tcruft pack instead of storing them as loose objects. `--cruft`\n"
"\tis on by default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:71
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--max-cruft-size=<n>::`\n"
"\tWhen packing unreachable objects into a cruft pack, limit the\n"
"\tsize of new cruft packs to be at most `<n>` bytes. Overrides any\n"
"\tvalue specified via the `gc.maxCruftSize` configuration. See\n"
"\tthe `--max-cruft-size` option of linkgit:git-repack[1] for\n"
"\tmore.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:79
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--prune=<date>::`\n"
"\tPrune loose objects older than date (default is 2 weeks ago,\n"
"\toverridable by the config variable `gc.pruneExpire`).\n"
"\t--prune=now prunes loose objects regardless of their age and\n"
"\tincreases the risk of corruption if another process is writing to\n"
"\tthe repository concurrently; see \"NOTES\" below. --prune is on by\n"
"\tdefault.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--no-prune::`\n"
"\tDo not prune any loose objects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--quiet::`\n"
"\tSuppress all progress reports.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:89
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--force::`\n"
"\tForce `git gc` to run even if there may be another `git gc`\n"
"\tinstance running on this repository.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--keep-largest-pack::`\n"
"\tAll packs except the largest non-cruft pack, any packs marked\n"
"\twith a `.keep` file, and any cruft pack(s) are consolidated into\n"
"\ta single pack. When this option is used, `gc.bigPackThreshold`\n"
"\tis ignored.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:97
#, no-wrap
msgid "AGGRESSIVE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:104
msgid ""
"When the `--aggressive` option is supplied, linkgit:git-repack[1] will be "
"invoked with the `-f` flag, which in turn will pass `--no-reuse-delta` to "
"linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]. This will throw away any existing deltas and "
"re-compute them, at the expense of spending much more time on the repacking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:109
msgid ""
"The effects of this are mostly persistent, e.g. when packs and loose objects "
"are coalesced into one another pack the existing deltas in that pack might "
"get re-used, but there are also various cases where we might pick a "
"sub-optimal delta from a newer pack instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:115
msgid ""
"Furthermore, supplying `--aggressive` will tweak the `--depth` and "
"`--window` options passed to linkgit:git-repack[1]. See the "
"`gc.aggressiveDepth` and `gc.aggressiveWindow` settings below. By using a "
"larger window size we're more likely to find more optimal deltas."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:121
msgid ""
"It's probably not worth it to use this option on a given repository without "
"running tailored performance benchmarks on it. It takes a lot more time, and "
"the resulting space/delta optimization may or may not be worth it. Not using "
"this at all is the right trade-off for most users and their repositories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "CONFIGURATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:130
#, no-wrap
msgid "NOTES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:143
msgid ""
"'git gc' tries very hard not to delete objects that are referenced anywhere "
"in your repository. In particular, it will keep not only objects referenced "
"by your current set of branches and tags, but also objects referenced by the "
"index, remote-tracking branches, reflogs (which may reference commits in "
"branches that were later amended or rewound), and anything else in the "
"refs/* namespace. Note that a note (of the kind created by 'git notes') "
"attached to an object does not contribute in keeping the object alive. If "
"you are expecting some objects to be deleted and they aren't, check all of "
"those locations and decide whether it makes sense in your case to remove "
"those references."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:150
msgid ""
"On the other hand, when 'git gc' runs concurrently with another process, "
"there is a risk of it deleting an object that the other process is using but "
"hasn't created a reference to. This may just cause the other process to fail "
"or may corrupt the repository if the other process later adds a reference to "
"the deleted object. Git has two features that significantly mitigate this "
"problem:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:153
msgid ""
"Any object with modification time newer than the `--prune` date is kept, "
"along with everything reachable from it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:157
msgid ""
"Most operations that add an object to the database update the modification "
"time of the object if it is already present so that #1 applies."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:161
msgid ""
"However, these features fall short of a complete solution, so users who run "
"commands concurrently have to live with some risk of corruption (which seems "
"to be low in practice)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:163
#, no-wrap
msgid "HOOKS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-gc.txt:167
msgid ""
"The 'git gc --auto' command will run the 'pre-auto-gc' hook.  See "
"linkgit:githooks[5] for more information."
msgstr ""

